{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"vibrant-legal-hind","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Context / Problem\nRebar currently resolves non-secret configuration through multiple authorities during one operation, while some backends ignore supplied config and reread ambient state. Secrets and authentication capabilities are also represented or propagated inconsistently across LLM, Git, bridge, MCP, review-bot, observability, and op-cert boundaries. This can change behavior mid-operation, discard an explicit Python/CLI choice, expose static material through repr/hash/subprocess siblings, or fail open through an unintended ambient/anonymous identity. RP-04 gives CLI, library, MCP, reconciler, and hosted services one application-wide composition contract without making Rebar a credential store.\n\nThe approved research and probes are recorded on `relevant-liberal-xantus` (`2704-39ae-16f5-4e96`) and `.joe-janitor/rp04-{composition-readback,secrets-research}.md`. They include a 17-input consumer census, five offline probe suites, Docker probes on three Linux images, 139 focused auth/security regressions, live Anthropic and Bedrock native-auth canaries, Gitleaks, and hosted workflow canaries.\n\n## Approach\nAt each operation boundary, resolve one immutable, serializable, non-secret `OperationSnapshot`. Precedence is explicit operation input > environment > selected project config > user config > central schema default. Resolve repository root separately as explicit `repo_root` > `REBAR_ROOT` > Git top-level/current directory, then interpret the winning relative paths against that root. CLI composes once per invocation; MCP and Python compose once per call (or Python accepts an already-composed snapshot); long-running processes additionally compose startup-owned deployment policy and live runtime bindings. An in-progress operation never rereads environment, files, cwd, or process-global config.\n\nCompose the snapshot with subsystem-local `GitRuntime`, `LLMRuntime`, and selected `BridgeRuntime` bindings. Provider SDKs/helpers remain authoritative for issuance, storage, refresh, expiry, invalidation, and precedence. Rebar-owned factories accept provider-native pre-client capabilities—such as a boto3 session, Anthropic access-token provider, or OpenAI workload-identity/callable-key provider—and construct/close the official client under Rebar retry, timeout, cache, output, error, and lifecycle policy. The initial public seam excludes arbitrary constructed clients/runners. Unavoidable static material uses provider-specific, non-serializable Pydantic `SecretStr`/`SecretBytes` carriers and is revealed only by the sending adapter. There is no universal `SecretProvider`, central credential union, automatic keyring, or application-owned cloud-secret retrieval.\n\nTrusted same-capability subprocesses inherit ambient environment for Git/SSH/AWS/proxy/CA compatibility. Rebar never installs adapter secrets by mutating global `os.environ`; it overlays a Rebar-materialized value only on its owning child and removes exact secret variable names declared by unrelated Rebar adapters from sibling child environments. Deployment step/container/service scoping remains the primary boundary, and the ADR must not claim process sandboxing.\n\nStatic bindings in long-running processes rotate on restart/redeploy; official refreshable providers retain their SDK-owned in-process lifecycle. Op-cert follows the same model: deployment supplies exactly one `REBAR_OPCERT_KEY_PATH` (preferred) or multiline `REBAR_OPCERT_PRIVATE_KEY`; startup follows mounted symlinks, accepts common 0444/0644 secret-volume modes, validates before queue/worker/network/workspace creation, and copies the key into a process-owned 0700 runtime directory/0600 file. Jobs receive an immutable context-local signer/principal/push-policy binding. Remove application `REBAR_OPCERT_SSM_KEY_PARAM`, `SsmKeyFetcher`, per-job provisioning, global environment patching, and runtime boto3/region coupling. Current deployment may retain ADR 0049 SSM-at-rest storage by extending `fetch-secrets.sh`, mounting the deployment source read-only, and using a 0700 runtime tmpfs.\n\nFail closed when an explicit, decision-bearing provider/profile/project/auth selection cannot be composed; never retry anonymously or through another ambient identity. Preserve the existing exception for optional write-only Langfuse/OTLP telemetry: absent, partial, or rejected observability auth disables tracing without changing the Rebar result.\n\n## Scope\nThis epic covers RP-04 only: central non-secret resolution and provenance, immutable operation projections, subsystem runtime bindings, provider-native auth injection, static-secret representation/propagation, op-cert startup composition, affected CLI/library/MCP/reconciler/hosted consumers, structural ownership gates, generated operator documentation, and migration canaries. RP-02/RP-03 reconciler semantics and RP-05/RP-06 remain separate janitor findings.\n\nIntentional behavior corrections are part of scope: explicit CLI input outranks environment for `tracker.dir`; malformed operation config fails before lock/network/write; backend properties stop changing after construction; supplied Python config is not ambiently re-overlaid; effective defaults move to the central schema; custom tracker directory/branch/remote reaches every owner; and the Jira Cloud write-side `DIG` fallback is removed in favor of explicit safety-critical project scope. Existing auth failure lessons, redaction, retry classification, TLS policy, and output contracts remain required unless a ticket names and tests an explicit behavior decision.\n\n## Migration / Rollback\nUse expand-contract sequencing: add snapshot/projection and runtime-binding types in shadow mode; prove cross-surface precedence, lifecycle, fingerprints, and non-leakage; adapt factories and consumers in call-graph slices behind compatibility facades; add structural ownership gates and generated docs; then run hosted canaries before removing legacy composition. Before write cutover, rollback disables shadow composition. During expansion, facades may delegate to legacy composition. After cutover, rollback targets a compatibility floor that understands the snapshot envelope; RP-04 changes no stored ticket or bridge format. Never persist or log credentials while diagnosing parity.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] CLI, MCP, Python, and direct reconciler compatibility paths derive equivalent non-secret behavior from one precedence/root contract, and a captured operation is stable across environment/file/cwd mutation while the next operation observes the change; verified by cross-surface behavioral tests.\n- [ ] Every affected config consumer receives a focused immutable projection or runtime binding, and an AST/source ownership gate reports zero ambient config/credential reads, backend factory/property reloads, or hard-coded configurable defaults below approved composition seams.\n- [ ] LLM, Git, and bridge integrations preserve provider-native auth lifecycles while constructing only the selected integration; missing or conflicting decision-bearing auth fails before network/write without anonymous, cross-provider, or alternate-principal fallback; verified by provider/client injection and current regression matrices.\n- [ ] Secret canaries are absent from repr, equality/hash, serialization, snapshots, fingerprints, caches, provenance, exceptions, logs, output, argv, URL/query strings, subprocess siblings, and CLI/MCP schemas; verified by boundary-specific behavioral tests rather than source-text proxies.\n- [ ] Trusted same-capability children preserve unknown ambient/native-chain variables, owning children receive only their adapter overlay, unrelated siblings lose exact Rebar-declared secret names, and concurrent operations never observe global environment mutation; verified by real subprocess and deterministic cross-thread tests.\n- [ ] Op-cert accepts exactly one startup path or multiline value, fails invalid composition before service work, uses a stable process-owned 0700/0600 copy and context-local signer, observes replacement only after restart, deletes only its copy, and has zero application runtime SSM/boto3/region dependency; verified by startup, restart, kill/tmpfs, concurrency, timeout, and existing producer-contract tests.\n- [ ] Generated config/security/operator documentation states precedence, operation/startup lifecycles, restart/redeploy rotation, provider ownership, the optional-observability exception, subprocess limitations, and the deliberate behavior deltas; drift checks fail when schema/docs diverge.\n- [ ] The migration removes legacy ambient/per-job composition only after focused suites and bounded live paths pass, with rollback procedures retaining accurate non-secret diagnostics.\n- [ ] [operator-attested] AWS review-bot and reconciler check/dry-run/bounded-live canaries pass after cutover without credential leakage or unauthorized fallback. provenance: record run/change identifiers, timestamps, selected provider/auth-source kind, retry counts, and redacted outcomes in epic comments.\n\n## Decomposition and sequencing\nThe ADR child records and critiques the approved composition boundary first. Implementation children are created only after that ADR completes HOLD/MERGE; they must be vertical, independently reviewable slices ordered by real produced/consumed artifacts. Each implementation child owns its behavioral oracles and exact file impact. The epic itself is not plan-reviewed or claimed for implementation until that graph exists, so an early ADR draft cannot be mistaken for permission to build.","id":"e215-ab23-ed6a-4791","parent_id":"","priority":1,"tags":["janitor","rp04","architecture","config","secrets","security"],"ticket_type":"epic","title":"RP-04 — Operation-scoped configuration and provider-auth composition"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1786990959694662001,"uuid":"f3564fb1-aecf-448d-a03e-f18d94b222fd"}